1
00:00:00,766 --> 00:00:01,933
April 2020

2
00:00:01,933 --> 00:00:03,733
A customer in Beijing posted on Weibo

3
00:00:03,733 --> 00:00:05,566
A photo of a Haidilao menu

4
00:00:05,766 --> 00:00:08,400
Half a portion of duck blood went from 16 to 23 yuan

5
00:00:08,433 --> 00:00:09,700
Eight small slices in total

6
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,533
Half a portion of potato slices was 13 yuan

7
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:13,600
That works out to 1.5 yuan per slice of potato

8
00:00:13,866 --> 00:00:15,400
A bowl of rice was 7 yuan

9
00:00:15,566 --> 00:00:17,966
A few days later Haidilao issued an apology

10
00:00:18,133 --> 00:00:20,300
Calling it a wrong decision by management

11
00:00:20,633 --> 00:00:22,566
All prices went back to pre-closure levels

12
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:24,966
My first reaction was the same as the comments

13
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:25,966
That's an insane price hike

14
00:00:26,166 --> 00:00:28,766
These past few days I dug up the April 2020 reports

15
00:00:28,766 --> 00:00:30,000
And read them again

16
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,100
Only to find I'd remembered one thing wrong from the start

17
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:35,033
Haidilao's overall price increase at the time

18
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:36,400
Was actually only 6%

19
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,300
Last episode we looked at another line

20
00:00:39,466 --> 00:00:43,033
Luxury brands raise prices twice a year, 10% each time

21
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:44,633
And people still queue outside

22
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:47,133
A 6% hike on hot pot got them bullied into an apology

23
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:49,366
A 10% hike on handbags made them sell even faster

24
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:51,766
Whether it's expensive isn't what this episode is about

25
00:00:51,966 --> 00:00:54,766
Honestly, when I put these two numbers side by side

26
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,300
I realized I'd been thinking in the wrong direction

27
00:00:57,466 --> 00:01:00,166
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics

28
00:01:00,166 --> 00:01:02,700
Number 8: price elasticity of demand

29
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:03,600
No memorizing definitions

30
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:04,833
Just one everyday calculation

31
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:06,000
To make it clear

32
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,966
How much the price goes up is one thing

33
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:12,900
How many people stay afterward is another

34
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,833
Economics counts these two separately

35
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:17,933
First, let's put this term in plain words

36
00:01:18,066 --> 00:01:19,933
Price elasticity of demand means

37
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,933
When price moves, how much does the quantity you buy move

38
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:26,366
Price goes up 10%, you buy 20% less

39
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:29,966
That's high elasticity, like a rubber band, it stretches when pulled

40
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:33,433
Price goes up 10%, you buy only 2% less

41
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:36,833
That's low elasticity, like a wire, it won't budge no matter how hard you pull

42
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:38,433
Last episode's law of demand

43
00:01:38,433 --> 00:01:40,533
Only told us the direction: pricier means buying less

44
00:01:40,666 --> 00:01:43,800
Elasticity tells us the size: how much less exactly

45
00:01:43,866 --> 00:01:47,500
Both numbers are just examples, not tied to any real product

46
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,733
But where most people actually get stuck

47
00:01:49,733 --> 00:01:51,200
Isn't in calculating the size

48
00:01:51,266 --> 00:01:54,566
It's in treating elasticity as a built-in property of the thing

49
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:57,733
Salt is wire, hot pot is rubber band

50
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:00,766
Luxury goods are wire, bubble tea is rubber band

51
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:03,966
By that logic Haidilao's apology is easy to explain

52
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:06,766
Hot pot is a rubber band, so you can't raise the price

53
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:08,366
That sounds perfectly reasonable

54
00:02:08,433 --> 00:02:11,300
But think back to the comments under that menu

55
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:13,833
Looking at the same 1.5 yuan slice of potato

56
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:15,866
Some said I'm never going back

57
00:02:15,866 --> 00:02:18,566
Others said it's not like I eat there every day, who cares

58
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:20,800
Same restaurant, same menu

59
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:23,966
Some people were rubber bands, others were wire

60
00:02:23,966 --> 00:02:26,166
So elasticity doesn't live in the hot pot

61
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,200
It lives in the people eating it

62
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Then what exactly

63
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,400
Decides whether someone is a rubber band or a wire

64
00:02:32,633 --> 00:02:34,366
In the end, what decides whether you walk away

65
00:02:34,366 --> 00:02:35,700
Isn't the dish on the table

66
00:02:36,066 --> 00:02:37,466
It's whether you push open the door

67
00:02:37,466 --> 00:02:39,233
And have somewhere else to go

68
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:42,166
Break that sentence down and you get three things

69
00:02:42,266 --> 00:02:44,566
First, is there somewhere else to go

70
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:47,000
There are ten hot pot restaurants downstairs from Haidilao

71
00:02:47,066 --> 00:02:49,966
But last episode's handbag was only sold at the boutique

72
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:53,033
Second, how big a chunk of your pocket is this

73
00:02:53,233 --> 00:02:54,800
A meal costing over 200 yuan per person

74
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:57,400
For someone making 5,000 a month versus 50,000 a month

75
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:58,933
Isn't the same money

76
00:02:59,366 --> 00:03:01,333
Third, do you have time to switch

77
00:03:01,633 --> 00:03:04,500
If you're hosting dinner tonight, you just have to take it

78
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:07,566
If you're meeting next month, you have all the time to shop around

79
00:03:07,866 --> 00:03:09,400
Substitutes, budget, time

80
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,433
None of them are written on the menu

81
00:03:11,566 --> 00:03:12,466
So how do these three things

82
00:03:12,466 --> 00:03:15,000
Work on a whole group of people at once

83
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:17,633
The clearest example I found is cigarettes

84
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:19,066
The International Agency for Research on Cancer

85
00:03:19,066 --> 00:03:20,966
Did a review in 2011

86
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,100
For every 10% increase in cigarette prices

87
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,266
Total consumption roughly

88
00:03:24,266 --> 00:03:26,233
Drops only 4% to 5%

89
00:03:26,433 --> 00:03:28,300
In the words we just used

90
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,366
Cigarettes are a very stiff wire

91
00:03:30,466 --> 00:03:32,733
Raise the price and most people stay

92
00:03:32,833 --> 00:03:35,133
That's why governments everywhere love tobacco taxes

93
00:03:35,366 --> 00:03:37,433
But break that 4 to 5 percent apart

94
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:39,333
And it's no longer a single number

95
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:41,900
Multiple studies reach the same conclusion

96
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,566
Young people's response to cigarette prices

97
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:45,300
Is two to three times that of adults

98
00:03:45,366 --> 00:03:48,233
Low-income people are also far more sensitive than high-income ones

99
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:51,500
Same price hike, longtime smokers are wire

100
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:53,966
The addiction is in their body, twenty years of smoking

101
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:56,166
No substitute and no time to switch

102
00:03:56,466 --> 00:03:58,100
Young people are rubber bands

103
00:03:58,233 --> 00:04:00,566
The addiction is still light and their pockets are thin

104
00:04:00,733 --> 00:04:03,100
A pack of cigarettes takes up a far bigger share of their budget

105
00:04:03,233 --> 00:04:06,366
So one tax hike hits two kinds of people at once

106
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:08,533
Those who stay pay more tax

107
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:11,033
Those not yet hooked simply never start

108
00:04:11,266 --> 00:04:14,100
Honestly, seeing these numbers is when I understood

109
00:04:14,133 --> 00:04:15,833
Among those 50 million people last episode

110
00:04:15,833 --> 00:04:18,333
Why young people left the most

111
00:04:18,333 --> 00:04:21,333
For them that handbag took too big a share of the budget

112
00:04:21,366 --> 00:04:22,733
And there were too many substitutes

113
00:04:22,966 --> 00:04:25,766
A bottle of perfume or a secondhand bag would do

114
00:04:28,533 --> 00:04:29,400
One more thing

115
00:04:29,966 --> 00:04:32,300
Elasticity isn't a fixed number stuck to a person

116
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:34,133
In 2011, Netflix

117
00:04:34,133 --> 00:04:36,500
Split streaming and DVDs into separate charges

118
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,033
Effectively a 60% price hike in one go

119
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:41,933
In one quarter it lost 800,000 US subscribers

120
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,200
Its stock fell more than 70% from its peak

121
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,700
From late 2023 to early 2025

122
00:04:46,766 --> 00:04:48,533
It raised prices several more times

123
00:04:48,733 --> 00:04:50,233
This time the users didn't run

124
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:53,366
In 2024 alone it added 41 million net subscribers

125
00:04:53,533 --> 00:04:54,900
Passing 300 million in total

126
00:04:55,066 --> 00:04:57,033
Same company, same move

127
00:04:57,166 --> 00:04:58,833
A dozen years apart, the opposite result

128
00:04:58,833 --> 00:05:00,033
What changed was the substitutes

129
00:05:00,033 --> 00:05:02,033
In 2011 you could always

130
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:03,100
Go back to the video rental store

131
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,733
Or watch cable TV

132
00:05:04,933 --> 00:05:06,066
Today what it takes up

133
00:05:06,066 --> 00:05:07,933
Is two hours of your daily habit

134
00:05:07,933 --> 00:05:09,400
And the shows are only on Netflix

135
00:05:09,533 --> 00:05:12,500
When substitutes disappear, the rubber band turns into wire

136
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:14,533
Now zoom out on this a bit

137
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:16,266
Elasticity is about quantity

138
00:05:16,266 --> 00:05:17,566
Businesses count money

139
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:19,933
Raise 10%, lose only 4%

140
00:05:19,933 --> 00:05:21,433
Revenue actually goes up

141
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:24,566
Raise 10%, lose 20%, and you lose money

142
00:05:24,766 --> 00:05:27,333
So when Haidilao raised prices 6%

143
00:05:27,466 --> 00:05:29,966
It was counting whether the extra paid by those who stayed

144
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:32,600
Was enough to cover those who left

145
00:05:32,833 --> 00:05:36,033
As long as fewer than 6% left, it wouldn't lose

146
00:05:36,166 --> 00:05:38,166
My guess is what it was really afraid of

147
00:05:38,166 --> 00:05:39,700
Wasn't losing a few percentage points

148
00:05:40,133 --> 00:05:43,166
It was how fast that menu was spreading on Weibo

149
00:05:43,166 --> 00:05:45,833
Put this on yourself and it works just as well

150
00:05:46,066 --> 00:05:47,666
Next time you get a price-increase notice

151
00:05:47,666 --> 00:05:49,600
Whether it's a video subscription, rent

152
00:05:49,633 --> 00:05:51,366
Or the barbershop you always go to

153
00:05:51,566 --> 00:05:53,933
Before getting angry, ask yourself one question

154
00:05:53,966 --> 00:05:55,600
Do I have a second option

155
00:05:55,833 --> 00:05:58,433
If yes, you can walk, and the business knows it

156
00:05:58,433 --> 00:05:59,733
It won't dare raise prices on you recklessly

157
00:05:59,966 --> 00:06:01,466
If no, you're the wire

158
00:06:01,466 --> 00:06:03,266
This price hike picked you

159
00:06:03,266 --> 00:06:06,233
Because when a business raises prices, it always picks the wire first

160
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,566
After writing this, I plan to do one small thing

161
00:06:08,666 --> 00:06:11,133
List every monthly auto-renewal I have

162
00:06:11,233 --> 00:06:13,900
And ask each one: do I have a second option

163
00:06:14,066 --> 00:06:15,666
The few I'd hate most to cancel

164
00:06:15,666 --> 00:06:17,833
Are exactly the ones I can't find a second option for

165
00:06:17,833 --> 00:06:19,933
What I still haven't figured out is

166
00:06:20,066 --> 00:06:22,100
Whether they're really worth the money

167
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:23,766
Or I'm just too lazy to look

168
00:06:23,966 --> 00:06:26,300
Back to that menu from April 2020

169
00:06:26,466 --> 00:06:28,000
1.5 yuan for a slice of potato

170
00:06:28,066 --> 00:06:30,566
What made people angry wasn't really the 1.5 yuan

171
00:06:30,733 --> 00:06:32,200
It's that everyone knows

172
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,300
Potatoes at the market cost a few yuan a kilo

173
00:06:34,366 --> 00:06:35,900
The substitute is right in front of you

174
00:06:36,133 --> 00:06:38,833
So this time, the customers were rubber bands

175
00:06:38,933 --> 00:06:40,766
Haidilao understood that

176
00:06:40,866 --> 00:06:42,900
And reversed the prices within days

177
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:45,566
When prices rise, fewer people buy

178
00:06:45,666 --> 00:06:46,900
But what about the sellers

179
00:06:47,033 --> 00:06:49,600
Next episode, we talk about the law of supply
